It's kinda by administrative fiat.  ;-)

That's not literally true, though, since the death mapping files are public.  

The file

	http://nethack.devnull.net/tournament/death_no.txt

contains game-ending notices which are not considered deaths.

The file

	http://nethack.devnull.net/tournament/death_yes.txt

contains a series of regular expressions for deaths which are considered
equivalent.  They're evaluated in sequence, so each death gets classed as
whichever pattern matches first.

Any decision we make, of course, can be challenged by the players and we'll
re-categorize anything for which someone puts forward a good argument.  ;-)

They're not decided in advance because it'd be way too much hassle to try to
guess all possible death notices in advance.  Since we start with last year's
categorized deaths, many of them are decided in advance, but not all.

The first statistic only refers to the particular player so, in that case, the
player has died 78 times and the system has decided that 37 are unique.  The
second statistic is system-wide, so it's saying we have 173 unique ways to die
listed so far.

-robin

